module bindings.java.bean_introspection_property_binding;

// `java.beans.Introspector.getBeanInfo(Class)` and the `BeanInfo` it returns
// are documented as handing back *all* of a class's properties -- declared and
// inherited, accessor-derived included. For `java.lang.Class` that set contains
// `classLoader` and `protectionDomain`, and from JDK 9 on it also contains
// `module`, whose own `classLoader` property belongs to a different bean class
// altogether. So the descriptor array an introspecting function receives has no
// security shape of its own: whether a name is safe to resolve is a property of
// the *type* that name reaches, and `PropertyDescriptor.getPropertyType()`
// paired with `Class.isAssignableFrom` is the API for asking it. `isAssignableFrom`
// rather than `==` because a container's loader is a subclass of
// `java.lang.ClassLoader`, never the class itself.
//
// The shape labelled here is the admission step. A function that takes the
// descriptor array and indexes every descriptor into a map under the
// descriptor's own name has just built a name-resolution surface: whatever it
// admits is reachable by that name from whoever consults the map. When the map
// backs a binder -- a `BeanWrapper`, a properties/XML/JSON mapper, a template or
// scripting bridge, an MBean surface -- the names arrive from outside the
// process, and an unbounded map hands each of them a reflective accessor. The
// classic instance is a binder that excluded the two `Class` properties by
// literal name and only for the one bean class `java.lang.Class`, so any path
// that reached a loader through another bean class -- `module`, or any property
// whose own type is a loader subclass -- resolved without a check.
//
// `getBeanInfo` is the load-bearing clause and it is keyed on the final segment
// so that both the static `Introspector.getBeanInfo(...)` and a receiverless
// helper that wraps it count. Without it the label lands on framework code whose
// `getPropertyDescriptors()` returns an unrelated class that merely shares the
// name: nifi's `org.apache.nifi.components.PropertyDescriptor` produced six
// function hits across three files in the measured candidate set before this
// clause was added, none of them within reach of `java.beans`. On the same
// candidate set the clause set below reports five functions in four trees, three
// of which are the same unbounded index built by other projects.
//
// Each of `call in [...]` is exact-token, and that is not stylistic:
// `containsAny` substring-matches the value after the prefix, so `["put"]` would
// also credit `putAll` and `["getName"]` would also credit `getNamedItem`, both
// of which occur in the measured candidate set.

binding unboundedBeanPropertyBinding {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.call in ["getBeanInfo"]
    and node.context.call in ["getPropertyDescriptors"]
    and node.context.call in ["put"]
    and node.context.call in ["getName"]
    and not (containsAny(node.context.call, ["isAssignableFrom"]))
  emit issue code.UnboundedBeanPropertyBinding at node
}
